    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.3",
    "topic_title": "Introduction to Macromolecules",
    "stem": "In a test tube, an enzyme completely breaks a single unbranched polymer made of 50 linked monomers into 50 free monomers. Which statement correctly describes this reaction?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Dehydration synthesis occurs; 49 water molecules are released as 49 covalent bonds between monomers are broken.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This mixes up the two reaction names. Dehydration synthesis forms bonds and releases water while building a polymer, but here the polymer is being broken into monomers. Fix: Ask first whether the molecule is getting larger or smaller, then match building to dehydration synthesis and breaking to hydrolysis."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Hydrolysis occurs; 49 water molecules are released as 49 covalent bonds between monomers are broken.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The reaction is named correctly, but the direction of water movement is reversed. Hydrolysis means splitting with water: water is added and consumed at each bond, not given off. Fix: Recall that hydrolysis adds water across a bond, and check that the water appears as a reactant, not a product."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Hydrolysis occurs; 49 water molecules are consumed as 49 covalent bonds between monomers are broken.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The polymer is being broken apart, so the reaction is hydrolysis. Each bond between monomers is cleaved by adding one water molecule, with H going to one monomer and OH to the other. An unbranched chain of 50 monomers has 49 linking bonds, so 49 water molecules are used."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Hydrolysis occurs; 50 water molecules are consumed as 50 covalent bonds between monomers are broken.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This counts monomers instead of the bonds between them. In an unbranched chain, the number of linking bonds is one less than the number of monomers, so only 49 bonds break and 49 waters are used. Fix: Count the bonds between monomers (n − 1 for a linear chain), and match one water to each bond."
     }
    ]
   },
